From 07c5baabf5cbf5d58f8aac2751625f436395acd1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Tigerblue77 <37409593+tigerblue77@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 20:17:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] Accept a leaf whose newest label no uberblock of its own
 covers

vdev_config_sync() writes labels 0 and 2 before the uberblocks of their
txg, so a crash in between leaves labels that no uberblock on the device
covers.  When the txg detaches one side of a two-way mirror, or the old
device of a replacing or spare vdev, those labels name the remaining
leaf by the guid of its parent, as the top-level vdev it was about to
become.  Labels written since the first commit of this series also
record its former guid, but labels written by older code do not:
vdev_validate() rejects the leaf against the MOS config, which still
names it by its own guid.  The pool imports degraded in place, and not
at all from devices that have moved, since the detached device has no
uberblocks left.  Nothing writes the rejected leaf, so the state stays
until the user repairs it by hand.

When the trusted config names a leaf by another guid than its newest
label, and that label is newer than every uberblock on the device, use
the newest label one of them covers, if it gives the same pool, vdev
and top-level guids as the trusted config.  The device never committed
the newer label, so it cannot override the ones before it.  The leaf
opens, a resilver covers what it missed while it was rejected, and the
import rewrites its labels.  If the device that carried the pool since
is missing, the pool now imports from this one at its older txg, as it
does from any stale side of a mirror, where it used to fail to import.

The import scan records each device under the guid of its first valid
label only.  Also note the guid of a valid label that disagrees, and use
it as a path of last resort, as for labels with txg 0, so that the leaf
is found when the devices have moved.

Signed-off-by: Tigerblue77 <37409593+tigerblue77@users.noreply.github.com>
